{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-109111" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-1,0),(1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+3xy\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109111" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/0dfe132146c4095954d6b628b055317c975fed6ec429b9ad48b68f4015d6c033.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=3xy.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+xy\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109111" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "230d0db5d162a7f30cd1c9371c9ca09f2c7608da3086d9df2651b7f7f46bc974"} {"question" "q-109111" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109111" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-y"] ["math" "3x"] ["math" "x-y"] ["math" "y"] ["math" "y-x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109111" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the quadrilateral $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(1,0),(1,1)$ and $(0,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,dx+2xy{e}^{xy}\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/b7fad52bdf54c0870ad4c4939a314f5657e390b589faf76e60a8d8ef5d926039.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}$ and $Q(x,y)=2xy{e}^{xy}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=(2y+x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=(2y+2x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,dx+2xy{e}^{xy}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[(2y+2x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy}-(2y+x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy}]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y+2x{y}^{2}-2y-x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "230d0db5d162a7f30cd1c9371c9ca09f2c7608da3086d9df2651b7f7f46bc974"} {"question" "q-109114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(2x-y){e}^{xy}"] ["math" "(xy-{y}^{2}){e}^{xy}"] ["math" "(y-2x){e}^{xy}"] ["math" "-x{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}"] ["math" "x{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-108588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the square $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(0,1),(1,0)$ and $(1,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+{x}^{2}\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise. Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-108588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/aa60dd35a920d166f7cc3238a366bde93bd2c33e1b39b0a38313ab46a8de2d8b.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+{x}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2(x-y)\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2(x-y).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-108588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "230d0db5d162a7f30cd1c9371c9ca09f2c7608da3086d9df2651b7f7f46bc974"} {"question" "q-108588" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-108588" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2(x+y)"] ["math" "2(x-y)"] ["math" "2(y-x)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}({y}^{3}+{x}^{3})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-108588" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2(x-y)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109161" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the path $C,$ in the plane, as the boundary of the region enclosed by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y\\,dx+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise. Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109161" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/c44b320dd7ba2824ae0024a3d77d0c6cd6e06fa5889e23879f3d6f464c3a80df.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y\\,dx+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y-(-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y)]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y+2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109161" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "230d0db5d162a7f30cd1c9371c9ca09f2c7608da3086d9df2651b7f7f46bc974"} {"question" "q-109161" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109161" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y)"] ["math" "3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y"] ["math" "{e}^{x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y)"] ["math" "{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y)"] ["math" "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109161" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the rectangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),(2,1)$ and $(0,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/8d57fb7f6b8e2e822a11f11a0267994c449c37b43df167a8d48ab8036854b8dd.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{x}^{2})]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)={x}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "230d0db5d162a7f30cd1c9371c9ca09f2c7608da3086d9df2651b7f7f46bc974"} {"question" "q-109112" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109112" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y"] ["math" "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2x"] ["math" "{x}^{2}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109112" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-42107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(0,-1)$ and $(1,0),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$  where $F(x,y)=⟨x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y⟩,$ as a repeated integral." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-42107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/6d4f13abad5cc193dccedee3114fbd9cbd9fb8e6b5b3ddb0d07f0ed2ed51f4ff.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le y+1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1+x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-42107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "230d0db5d162a7f30cd1c9371c9ca09f2c7608da3086d9df2651b7f7f46bc974"} {"question" "q-42107" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-42107" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1+x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1+x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y}(1+x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}(1+x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-42107" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1+x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the unit circle and the coordinate axes in the first quadrant. Express ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$  where $F(x,y)=⟨y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,x-{e}^{-{y}^{2}}⟩,$ as a repeated integral." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/e48034aed1f41cf1f9b30c057186879233fe05cd2ba720dcee436398a732b6ce.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx+(x-{e}^{-{y}^{2}})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=x-{e}^{-{y}^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx+(x-{e}^{-{y}^{2}})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "230d0db5d162a7f30cd1c9371c9ca09f2c7608da3086d9df2651b7f7f46bc974"} {"question" "q-109254" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109254" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109254" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=x+2.$ Express ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$  where $F(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2},{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}⟩,$ as a repeated integral." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/e32b8e7d6ce80d7cedad928f9a2f7acc3c3d7f06add7909e856db27d154068d6.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "230d0db5d162a7f30cd1c9371c9ca09f2c7608da3086d9df2651b7f7f46bc974"} {"question" "q-109263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{x+2}^{{x}^{2}+x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}(2y-x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,-1),(1,0)$ and $(0,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$  where $F(x,y)=⟨{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x⟩,$ as a repeated integral." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/c2e08c233cb31124234ab6e5179998a3bdc148024632c7770bdea46cd37dff83.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx+(xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y+2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x-1\\le y\\le -2x+2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx+(xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{-2x+2}y\\text{ }dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "230d0db5d162a7f30cd1c9371c9ca09f2c7608da3086d9df2651b7f7f46bc974"} {"question" "q-109291" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109291" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{x+1}^{-2x+2}y\\text{ }dy\\,dx"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{-2x+2}y\\text{ }dy\\,dx"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{-2x+2}(x-(2+{y}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }dy\\,dx"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{-2x+2}(y+4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{ }dy\\,dx"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{-2x+2}y\\text{ }dy\\,dx"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109291" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{-2x+2}y\\text{ }dy\\,dx"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-108467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(1,0)$ and $(1,2),$ traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-108467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/2e63a57e50b60d7f0c6a3ce12d164716e2cba71c6bd4c6df10460a35beba4209.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 2x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-108467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "230d0db5d162a7f30cd1c9371c9ca09f2c7608da3086d9df2651b7f7f46bc974"} {"question" "q-108467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-108467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}2x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-108467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}(2y-x)\\,dx+(5x-y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=x$ and $y={x}^{2}-x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/e788bbcbda4cc715c6989b7de9d08d60e3b0d178eeb9432a342a4c86c4c01b86.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2y-x$ and $Q(x,y)=5x-y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}-x\\le y\\le x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2y-x)\\,dx+(5x-y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5-2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}-x}^{x}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}[y]_{{x}^{2}-x}^{x}\\,dx \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-{x}^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & =3{[{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3(4-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "230d0db5d162a7f30cd1c9371c9ca09f2c7608da3086d9df2651b7f7f46bc974"} {"question" "q-109160" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109160" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{7}{3}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109160" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109164" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}2xy\\,dx+{y}^{2}\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}$ and $y=\\sqrt{3x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109164" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/eb0b120cc4d63c8dab18c8acb37f5835e99db746584262f6bb33e8ca99b6a16d.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2xy$ and $Q(x,y)={y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{3x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}2xy\\,dx+{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(0-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}/3}^{\\sqrt{3x}}(-2x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2x)[y]_{{x}^{2}/3}^{\\sqrt{3x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2x)(\\sqrt{3x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})dx \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\sqrt{3}{x}^{3/2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})dx \\\\  & =-2{[\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{3x}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{12}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{2(3)^{2}\\sqrt{3(3)}}{5}-\\frac{(3)^{4}}{12}) \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{54}{5}-\\frac{27}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{81}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109164" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "230d0db5d162a7f30cd1c9371c9ca09f2c7608da3086d9df2651b7f7f46bc974"} {"question" "q-109164" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109164" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-9"] ["math" "-\\frac{81}{10}"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\frac{23}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{41}{20}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109164" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{81}{10}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109147" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+({e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2})\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(2,2)$ and $(-2,2),$ traversed counterclockwise." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109147" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/7ca4b6fe3a5a16c4004c75b0e4524cc3b42550cb2413d5b8e1c21a1d53cee902.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-y\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+({e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{-y}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3})dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{3}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{[{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109147" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "230d0db5d162a7f30cd1c9371c9ca09f2c7608da3086d9df2651b7f7f46bc974"} {"question" "q-109147" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109147" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-18"] ["math" "-\\frac{32}{9}"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "\\frac{16}{27}"] ["math" "\\frac{64}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109147" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{64}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-41811" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{3}+2{y}^{2},{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(2,0)$ and $(1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-41811" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/73bf9b973e50c540a873f4f1a44d936474e4c49d7041a330902508a840645009.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({x}^{3}+2{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{3}+2{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):y\\le x\\le 2-y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{3}+2{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x-4y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}(2x-4y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}-4xy]}_{y}^{2-y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(2-y)^{2}-4(2-y)y-{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{2}-12y+4)\\,dy \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{y}^{2}-3y+1)\\,dy \\\\  & =4{[\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}+y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{3}{2}+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-41811" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "230d0db5d162a7f30cd1c9371c9ca09f2c7608da3086d9df2651b7f7f46bc974"} {"question" "q-41811" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-41811" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2}{5}"] ["math" "-\\frac{8}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-41811" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=(3xy-1)\\,i+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,j$ and $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=2x$ and $y={x}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/5f34ac74599937e0ae181126ff9a3da5b29dfe213614a1f23ca10c7062f22f7d.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(3xy-1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3xy-1$ and $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(3xy-1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6x-3x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}3x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3x[y]_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3x(2x-{x}^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{x}^{2}-3{x}^{3})\\,dx \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{3}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2(2)^{3}-\\frac{3(2)^{4}}{4} \\\\  & =16-12 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "230d0db5d162a7f30cd1c9371c9ca09f2c7608da3086d9df2651b7f7f46bc974"} {"question" "q-109115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-7"] ["math" "-\\frac{15}{4}"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "\\frac{11}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "q-109115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15198fcc60e3e449698d7fc754bcb5deffa13a31edf7643b3a319297c6756bb"} {"question" "e-9071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0), (1,0)$ and $(0,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\cos{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+x\\sin{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y}= \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\,\\textrm d A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise. Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/example-9071.json" "file_sha256" "d67fd1734cd33428d5203db46c055f33dbd14434ad95a576e9ab2b80331da704"} {"question" "e-9071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/a78a98750e3203760d27daaebd42852198538935e2c3777a0b8a8c85d1bb0901.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)= \\cos{y}$ and $Q(x,y)= x\\sin{y}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y) = 2\\sin{y}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/example-9071.json" "file_sha256" "d67fd1734cd33428d5203db46c055f33dbd14434ad95a576e9ab2b80331da704"} {"question" "e-9086" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0), (1,1)$ and $(-1,1),$ traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}x\\sin{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+ (x^2\\cos{y}-y^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}y}$ as a repeated integral." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/example-9086.json" "file_sha256" "9fd19bbe3788168e8b2306fdcfba5a89c9d35819fc613e97bf52b2e3fbaf1a78"} {"question" "e-9086" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/2fa3c84359cec5f5e0750552e5a4b24b20b8dbc3d65b909aea908e1505cfb5e3.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)= x\\sin{y}$ and $Q(x,y)= x^2\\cos{y}-y^2.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y) :  -y\\leq x\\leq y,\\, 0\\leq y \\leq 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dx+({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/example-9086.json" "file_sha256" "9fd19bbe3788168e8b2306fdcfba5a89c9d35819fc613e97bf52b2e3fbaf1a78"} {"question" "e-9072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C} 4x^2y \\, \\mathrm{d}x+ 2y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y},$ where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(1,2)$ and $(0,2),$ traversed counterclockwise." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/example-9072.json" "file_sha256" "f48b0b3ffa39d49ed8d4ae1abe889fa1039cd67fb9d33d5a7254cb02adc8df2e"} {"question" "e-9072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/a87f696b1eb42588ab69800b72d821bf47b35676fc19dcd4fe2927bbf36ba3f8.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)= 4x^2y$ and $Q(x,y)= 2y^2.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) : 0 \\leq x \\leq 1,\\, 2x \\leq y \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}4{x}^{2}y\\,dx+2{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(0-4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2x}^{2}(-4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}[y]_{2x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(2-2x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(1-x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}\\,dx \\\\  & =-8{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-8(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{12} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/example-9072.json" "file_sha256" "f48b0b3ffa39d49ed8d4ae1abe889fa1039cd67fb9d33d5a7254cb02adc8df2e"}] "usage" {"e-9086" [] "q-109147" [] "q-109161" [] "e-9071" [] "q-108467" [] "e-9072" [] "q-108588" []} "basis" 1966 "retractions" [[17592186133991 :question/problem "Consider the square $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0, 0), (0, 1), (1, 0)$ and $(1, 1)$, and the region $D$ bounded by $C$. According to Green's theorem,\n\n$∮_(C)y^{2}dx + x^{2}dy = ∬_(D)f(x, y)dA$,\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise. Calculate the function $f(x, y)$."] [17592186133991 :question/answer-fields 17592186130509] [17592186133997 :question/problem "Consider the path $C$, in the plane, as the boundary of the region enclosed by the circle $x^{2} + y^{2} = 1$, and the region $D$ bounded by $C$. According to Green's theorem,\n\n$∮_(C)e^{x}\\cos 2ydx + e^{x}\\sin 2ydy = ∬_(D)f(x, y)dA$,\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise. Calculate the function $f(x, y)$."] [17592186133990 :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0, 0), (1, 0)$ and $(1, 2)$, traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C$. Express $∮_(C)xy^{2}dx + 2x^{2}ydy$ as a repeated integral."] [17592186133996 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $∮_(C)(e^{x} - 2x^{2}y)dx + (e^{y} + 2xy^{2})dy$, where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0, 0)$, $(2, 2)$ and $(-2, 2)$, traversed counterclockwise."] [17592186133621 :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0), (1,0)$ and $(0,2)$, and the region $D$ bounded by $C$. According to Green's theorem,\n$\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\cos{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+x\\sin{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y}= \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\,\\textrm d A$,\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise. Calculate the function $f(x,y)$."] [17592186133621 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C$. If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2116/Source/Images/db911dc99f33b91c66fb04d8b1e4301f.png)\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nP(x,y)= \\cos{y}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y)= x\\sin{y}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} =-\\sin y, \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} = \\sin y\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)\\cos ydx + x\\sin ydy &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(\\sin y - (-\\sin y)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)2\\sin ydA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2\\sin{y}\n$$"] [17592186133623 :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0), (1,1)$ and $(-1,1)$, traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C$. Express $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}x\\sin{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+ (x^2\\cos{y}-y^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}y}$ as a repeated integral."] [17592186133623 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C$. If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2116/Source/Images/d31d071642487b14587e48bac2c69b6a.png)\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nP(x,y)= x\\sin{y}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y)= x^2\\cos{y}-y^2\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} = x\\cos y, \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} = 2x\\cos y\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y): -y\\leq x\\leq y,\\, 0\\leq y \\leq 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)x\\sin ydx + (x^{2}\\cos y - y^{2})dy &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(2x\\cos y - x\\cos y)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)x\\cos ydA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{-y}^{y}x\\cos ydxdy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133622 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C} 4x^2y \\, \\mathrm{d}x+ 2y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y}$, where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0)$, $(1,2)$ and $(0,2)$, traversed counterclockwise."] [17592186133622 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C$. If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2116/Source/Images/bdec15081a2e1ef58dcb3545c680a71d.png)\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nP(x,y)= 4x^2y\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y)= 2y^2\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} = 4x^{2}, \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y): 0 \\leq x \\leq 1,\\, 2x \\leq y \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)4x^{2}ydx + 2y^{2}dy &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(0 - 4x^{2})dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{2x}^{2}(-4x^{2})dydx \\\\\n&=-4∫_{0}^{1}x^{2}[y]_{2x}^{2}dx \\\\\n&=-4∫_{0}^{1}x^{2}(2 - 2x)dx \\\\\n&=-8∫_{0}^{1}x^{2}(1 - x)dx \\\\\n&=-8∫_{0}^{1}x^{2} - x^{3}dx \\\\\n&=-8[\\frac{x^{3}}{3} - \\frac{x^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&=-8(\\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\\n&=-8 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186130504 17592186130505 17592186130506 17592186130507 17592186130508 17592186130510 17592186130511 17592186130512 17592186130513 17592186130514 17592186130540 17592186130541 17592186130542 17592186130543 17592186130544 17592186130546 17592186130547 17592186130548 17592186130549 17592186130550] "immutable_fields" [[17592186130503 :answer-field/correct] [17592186130503 :answer-field/id] [17592186130503 :answer-field/key] [1759218613